<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mamallapuram</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th–8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Mahabalipuram</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mamallapuram, or Mahabalipuram, is a coastal town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, that was a port of the Pallava dynasty and is named after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-i">Narasimhavarman I</a>, called Mamalla. Its monuments, a World Heritage Site, include the monolithic rathas, cave temples, the great relief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/descent-of-the-ganges">Arjuna's Penance</a> and the Shore Temple built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-ii">Narasimhavarman II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mamallapuram.jpg" alt="Mamallapuram" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_collage_of_Mamallapuram_town_Tamil_Nadu_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
